TunnelOS
A Computational Infrastructure Platform for Data-Driven Tunnel Construction

TunnelOS is a proposed software architecture for the digitisation, observation, simulation and optimisation of tunnel-boring operations. It is designed around a three-layer computational model:

Rust for real-time industrial telemetry and edge processing;
Python for simulation, statistical analysis and optimisation;
Kotlin for the operator-facing control and visualisation environment.

The central proposition is that tunnel construction can be represented as a continuously observed cyber-physical system, in which machine telemetry, geological information, construction progress, maintenance state and project economics are brought into a common computational model.

TunnelOS is therefore conceived not as a conventional dashboard, but as a software infrastructure layer for underground construction.

1. Executive Thesis

Tunnel boring is an unusually data-intensive engineering process.

A modern TBM continuously generates measurements relating to thrust, torque, cutterhead rotation, pressure, temperature, vibration, penetration, machine state and production. At the same time, the construction environment produces independent streams of geological, geotechnical, surveying, instrumentation and project-management information.

The engineering problem is consequently not simply one of data acquisition. It is one of data integration, temporal inference, uncertainty management and decision support.

TunnelOS proposes an architecture in which these information streams become computationally related:

                  TUNNELOS
                     │
        ┌────────────┼────────────┐
        │            │            │
     MACHINE      GEOLOGY      PROJECT
      DATA         DATA         DATA
        │            │            │
        └────────────┼────────────┘
                     ↓
              DIGITAL TWIN
                     ↓
        ┌────────────┼────────────┐
        ↓            ↓            ↓
   SIMULATION   OPTIMISATION   FORECASTING
        │            │            │
        └────────────┼────────────┘
                     ↓
             ENGINEERING DECISION

The investment thesis is therefore based upon the creation of a specialised computational infrastructure layer for tunnelling, rather than another general-purpose construction-management application.

2. The Problem

Underground construction presents a particularly difficult computational environment because the system being operated is only partially observable.

The operator can measure machine behaviour continuously, while geological conditions ahead of the cutting face are inherently uncertain.

Consequently:

Observed Machine State
        +
Historical Data
        +
Geological Model
        +
Instrumentation
        +
Engineering Knowledge
        ↓
Probabilistic Understanding
        ↓
Engineering Decision

TBM operational data has consequently become an important source of information for understanding machine performance and, potentially, geological conditions. Academic research has demonstrated the use of TBM operating data for geological prediction and for optimisation of operating parameters.

The underlying opportunity is to transform this fragmented information environment into a coherent computational system.

3. The TunnelOS Proposition

TunnelOS seeks to provide five capabilities within one architecture:

Real-time telemetry
Digital representation of the tunnel and TBM
Simulation
Engineering optimisation
Predictive analytics

The resulting system can be understood as a feedback architecture:

             PHYSICAL WORLD
                   │
                   ↓
              TBM / SENSORS
                   │
                   ↓
          REAL-TIME TELEMETRY
                   │
                   ↓
              DIGITAL TWIN
                   │
             ┌─────┴─────┐
             ↓           ↓
        SIMULATION    ANALYTICS
             │           │
             └─────┬─────┘
                   ↓
             OPTIMISATION
                   │
                   ↓
            HUMAN ENGINEER
                   │
                   ↓
            PHYSICAL SYSTEM

The important architectural distinction is that TunnelOS is designed to inform and augment engineering decisions, rather than initially attempting to replace certified machine-control systems.

4. Technical Architecture

TunnelOS consists of three principal computational layers.

Rust — Industrial Telemetry

Rust forms the real-time data-acquisition and edge-computing layer.

TBM / PLC / Sensors
        ↓
Industrial Protocols
        ↓
Rust Telemetry Engine
        ↓
Validation
        ↓
Normalisation
        ↓
Buffering
        ↓
Event Stream

The Rust layer is intended to provide:

High-throughput telemetry ingestion
Deterministic data processing
Sensor validation
Timestamping
Event generation
Local buffering
Connection management
Real-time streaming
Machine-state reconstruction

It can interface with appropriate industrial protocols and downstream systems without making the higher-level applications responsible for raw machine communication.

Python — Analytical Engine

Python constitutes the computational research and analytical layer.

It is intended to provide:

TBM simulation
Digital-twin modelling
Geological modelling
Statistical analysis
Optimisation
Energy modelling
Cutter-wear analysis
Predictive maintenance
Production forecasting
Project economics

The conceptual loop is:

TELEMETRY
    ↓
STATE ESTIMATION
    ↓
SIMULATION
    ↓
OPTIMISATION
    ↓
FORECAST
    ↓
ENGINEERING RECOMMENDATION

Importantly, optimisation outputs are recommendations rather than uncontrolled actuator commands.

Kotlin — Operational Interface

Kotlin provides the human-facing computational environment.

The interface is designed for:

Control rooms
Engineering offices
Site management
Tablets
Desktop workstations

It provides:

Live telemetry
TBM state
Tunnel visualisation
Geological profiles
Alerts
Machine health
Production metrics
Historical analysis
Simulation results

The interface therefore becomes the human-readable representation of the digital twin.

5. Digital Twin

The central abstraction within TunnelOS is the tunnel digital twin.

It represents the relationship between:

TBM
│
├── Machine State
├── Mechanical State
├── Electrical State
├── Hydraulic State
├── Cutter State
│
├── Position
├── Chainage
└── Production

TUNNEL
│
├── Geometry
├── Alignment
├── Lining
├── Geological Zones
└── Infrastructure

ENVIRONMENT
│
├── Ground Conditions
├── Groundwater
├── Instrumentation
└── External Constraints

The purpose is not merely visualisation.

The digital twin provides the state space within which analytical models operate.

Digital-twin research increasingly identifies this combination of continuously updated physical state, computational models and predictive maintenance as an important industrial architecture, while also highlighting unresolved problems surrounding explainability, standardisation and generalisability.

TunnelOS therefore treats the digital twin as an engineering model rather than a marketing visualisation.

6. Geological Intelligence

One of the principal sources of uncertainty in tunnelling is the condition of the ground ahead of the excavation face.

TunnelOS is designed to combine:

Geological surveys
Borehole information
Geotechnical measurements
TBM telemetry
Historical excavation data
Ground instrumentation
Engineering interpretation

into a spatial and temporal geological model.

A conceptual representation is:

CHAINAGE

0 ─────── 2,000 ─────── 4,000 ─────── 6,000m
          │                │
        ROCK            MIXED GROUND
                           │
                       WATER RISK

The system can subsequently compare predicted and observed conditions.

This creates an important feedback mechanism:

PREDICTED GEOLOGY
       ↓
TBM OPERATES
       ↓
TELEMETRY OBSERVED
       ↓
GROUND CONDITIONS INFERRED
       ↓
MODEL UPDATED

Research has demonstrated that TBM operational data can be used as an additional source for geological prediction, particularly over short ranges ahead of the machine.

7. Optimisation

TunnelOS is intended to treat TBM operation as a constrained optimisation problem.

A simplified objective function can be expressed conceptually as:

MAXIMISE

    excavation productivity

SUBJECT TO

    machine constraints
    energy consumption
    cutter wear
    thermal constraints
    geological conditions
    operational constraints

The system can therefore evaluate alternative scenarios:

BASELINE
      │
      ├── HIGH PRODUCTIVITY
      │
      ├── LOW ENERGY
      │
      ├── LOW WEAR
      │
      └── LOW RISK

Academic work has already demonstrated the feasibility of multi-objective TBM operating-parameter optimisation under engineering constraints, including field validation in published research.

TunnelOS seeks to provide an extensible software architecture around this class of problem.

8. Predictive Maintenance

Machine downtime represents a potentially significant economic variable in tunnel construction.

TunnelOS therefore models component health as a temporal data problem.

TEMPERATURE
     +
VIBRATION
     +
PRESSURE
     +
POWER
     +
OPERATING HOURS
     ↓
HEALTH MODEL
     ↓
ANOMALY DETECTION
     ↓
MAINTENANCE FORECAST

The architecture deliberately favours interpretable engineering models before introducing opaque machine-learning systems.

This distinction is important.

A technically sophisticated industrial platform should not confuse:

prediction

with

understanding.

Machine learning can subsequently be introduced where sufficient validated historical data exists.

9. Industrial Telemetry

The Rust layer is intended to provide a high-integrity telemetry pipeline:

RECEIVE
   ↓
PARSE
   ↓
VALIDATE
   ↓
NORMALISE
   ↓
TIMESTAMP
   ↓
BUFFER
   ↓
STREAM
   ↓
STORE

Each observation should carry metadata describing:

Source
Timestamp
Sensor
Measurement
Unit
Quality
Sequence
Machine identity

Telemetry should be explicitly classified as:

VALID
SUSPECT
INVALID
STALE
DISCONNECTED
SIMULATED

This is important because industrial analytics are only as reliable as their underlying measurement provenance.

10. Cybersecurity and Safety Philosophy

TunnelOS is designed around a deliberate separation between monitoring/analytics and certified machine control.

TunnelOS
    │
    │ OBSERVES
    ↓
Industrial Control System
    │
    │ CONTROLS
    ↓
TBM

TunnelOS does not assume the role of a safety PLC.

It should not:

Override emergency stops
Modify certified safety limits
Bypass manufacturer controls
Directly operate critical actuators
Convert analytical recommendations into autonomous machine commands without an appropriately engineered control layer

This architectural separation is consistent with the special security, reliability and safety requirements associated with industrial control systems identified by NIST.

Cybersecurity is consequently treated as an architectural property rather than an additional feature.

11. Information Architecture

TunnelOS is designed to operate across the information lifecycle of a tunnel project.

DESIGN
  ↓
SITE INVESTIGATION
  ↓
CONSTRUCTION
  ↓
EXCAVATION
  ↓
MONITORING
  ↓
COMMISSIONING
  ↓
OPERATION
  ↓
MAINTENANCE

This is consistent with the broader direction of structured information management in the built environment, where ISO 19650 addresses information exchange, recording, versioning and organisation across the asset lifecycle.

The long-term opportunity is therefore potentially broader than TBM telemetry alone.

12. Data Model

The conceptual data architecture is:

PROJECT
│
├── TUNNEL
│   ├── ALIGNMENT
│   ├── SECTIONS
│   ├── GEOLOGY
│   └── LINING
│
├── TBM
│   ├── TELEMETRY
│   ├── COMPONENTS
│   ├── MAINTENANCE
│   └── EVENTS
│
├── INSTRUMENTATION
│
├── SIMULATIONS
│
├── OPTIMISATION RUNS
│
└── ENGINEERING DECISIONS

This creates a persistent computational record of the project.

13. Event-Sourced Engineering History

TunnelOS should preserve the history of significant machine and engineering events.

For example:

08:41  TBM STARTED

09:17  ADVANCE RATE INCREASED

09:43  GEOLOGICAL TRANSITION

10:02  TORQUE INCREASE

10:18  VIBRATION WARNING

10:31  TBM PAUSED

11:04  INSPECTION COMPLETED

11:37  TBM RESUMED

This enables retrospective analysis of the relationship between:

ground → machine → intervention → outcome.

Such historical relationships are potentially valuable intellectual and commercial assets.

14. Competitive Positioning

TunnelOS should not be understood as merely:

A dashboard
A SCADA replacement
A project-management system
A generic IoT platform
An AI chatbot for engineers

Its intended position is between these categories:

INDUSTRIAL DATA
       +
GEOTECHNICAL DATA
       +
DIGITAL TWIN
       +
SIMULATION
       +
OPTIMISATION
       +
ENGINEERING WORKFLOW

The market already demonstrates demand for real-time tunnel monitoring and integrated TBM/geotechnical information systems; existing platforms provide evidence that construction telemetry, instrumentation, BIM visualisation, analytics and TBM dashboards can form a commercially valuable software category.

TunnelOS therefore should compete through architecture, interoperability, analytical depth and extensibility, rather than through the simple novelty of collecting machine data.

